== A Master’s Employing and Rewarding of His Servants

=== §. 35. #emph[Of employing servants well.]

The charge which the master gave to his servants when he was going away (“Conduct business with this until I return”)#footnote[Luke 19:13.] shows that masters must keep their servants exercised and employed in some business or other. This is also implied in this part of a good mistress’s commendation, “she gives a portion to her maidservants,” #footnote[Prov 31:15.] meaning a portion of work. As there is never an idle member in a natural body, but every one is employed, so it should be in a family.

1\. Servants thus accustomed to hard work while they are in subjection will be more industrious when they are their own masters. Indeed, they will both more willingly undertake, and more easily carry through, matters which require pains and diligence. #emph[Practice makes perfect.]

2\. In this way masters themselves, and others afterward, will receive more profit and greater benefit from them.

3\. In this way many temptations will be avoided and evils prevented. Continual employment is to the corrupt nature of man like a running stream which carries away all the mud and filth in a brook, so that none settles there.

Contrary to this is sheltering idlers in one’s #footnote[Contrary.] house, giving food and drink and lodging to such as do no work at all. The apostle expressly commanded, “If #footnote[2 Thess 3:10.] anyone is unwilling to work, he shall not eat.”

These are not only a prey to the devil, but also instruments of the devil, by which he works much harm. For these are the ones most ready to reveal the secrets of a house, to gossip about everything that is done, and to fall in with any company; and, not content to do no good themselves, they disturb, interrupt and draw away those who are busy at their work.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of exercising servants in a calling.]

Though it is a good thing to keep a servant always occupied and employed, yet for the servant’s benefit it is further needful that his employment is about some settled matter, in which he may also exercise himself when he has left service. This especially concerns those who have taken #footnote[Masters must teach their apprentices their trade.] apprentices. They must teach them their trade.

+ For that end apprentices are bound to masters.
+ The terms of the agreement on the master’s part require as much.
+ The good likely to come from it to the master himself, his apprentice and others will repay the pains.

Other servants also must be kept to a work which may be a means of maintenance: in a great house, to duties about that house; in the country, to farming; in legal offices, to some employment there; and so in other callings.

Contrary to this is masters’ begrudging their apprentices the secrets of their trade, and employing them time after time on messages and errands and such things as serve only the master’s present need, but cannot profit the servants in time to come. These masters are like old, full-grown, broad trees, which keep all the sunshine from the shrubs that grow under them, and so keep them from growing.

#emph[Objection.] Apprentices will be like ivy to the trees they cling to, soon overtopping them and soaking all the life out of them. They will hinder their master’s trade and draw away all his customers if they become too expert in his trade.

#emph[Answer.] 1. This is only a surmise. It implies that masters who fear what is alleged do not deal as honestly and faithfully with their customers as they should. Otherwise, how could they suspect that wise buyers would leave one of whom they have had long and good experience to go to a beginner?

2\. Daily experience shows that God by his providence so orders men’s affairs that masters who from time to time train up and send out many apprentices, well practiced and skillful in their trade, hold their own, and indeed increase in their own dealings and in the gain they get by them; and yet at the same time their apprentices also do well. Why should any masters so distrust God’s providence as to be afraid to make their apprentices skillful in their trade?

3\. When masters give up trading, by death or otherwise, how will trades be continued if masters are so envious and distrustful? What if their own masters had dealt with them like that? And what if all masters dealt like that? For what one does in such a case, he must suppose that all may do.

Those mistresses also offend in this way who keep their maids at drudgery for many years on end, and never teach them, nor give them the means or the leisure to learn the skills of household management, things by which they may earn a better living for themselves.

Such masters and mistresses use their servants like beasts, only to serve their own ends, without any regard for the servants’ good. In this they pervert the main end of the relation between master and servant, which is a mutual and reciprocal good passing from the one to the other.

=== §. 37. #emph[Of appointing to every servant his particular function.]

For the better exercising of servants in some calling, let these #footnote[Directions for fitting servants to some function.] directions be noted.

1\. That masters assign their servants their own distinct work, so that they may know what to do and in what to exercise themselves; and if there are many servants in one house, that they set each one his distinct function. The phrase noted before of the good mistress (“she gives a portion to her maidservants”) #footnote[Prov 31:15.] proves this much. This was one thing which the Queen of Sheba observed and marveled at in Solomon’s house, “the standing of #footnote[1 Kgs 10:5.] his servants,” namely, each one in his own place, at his own task. To this purpose it is noted that the great master gave to #footnote[Matt 25:15.] each of his servants their distinct talents. It is like this in the natural body: every member is not only employed, but also employed #footnote[1 Cor 12:8. #emph[etc. Quod plurimorum commune est, in eo procurando minima diligentia adhibetur. Rerum enim propriarum maximam curam habent, communium autem minorem. Arist. de Repub. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 3. \[What is common to most receives the least care in looking after it; for men take the greatest care of their own things, and less of what is common.\]] in its own function: the eye in seeing, the ear in hearing, the foot in standing or walking, and so with the rest. It is like this also in Christ’s mystical body: one has the spirit of #emph[wisdom,] another of #emph[knowledge,] another of #emph[faith,] and so with other gifts. So also it ought to be in a family where there are many servants.

This is an especially good means to make each one more diligent and faithful. For when each one has his particular work, they know that they in particular are to give an account of it. So if it is not done, or done badly, they will bear all the blame; if it is done, and done well, they will have all the praise.

2\. In this way each one will be made skillful and expert in something, by constantly attending to it and exercising themselves in it.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of disorder in families through masters’ negligence.]

The great disorder in many families is contrary to this. For there are many masters who, having a number of servants, expect everything to be done well, and yet appoint no particular place or work to anyone, but think and say that everyone should be eager to do everything. And when they find #footnote[Praeter alias causas eo sunt in rebus communibus negligentiores, quod eas alijs curae esse existiment: quemadmodum euenire solet in ministerijs servilibus. Arist. loc. citat.] things not done to their liking, they fret and fume, and complain that they keep so many servants and yet nothing is done well. Such servants are not free from all blame; but surely the masters have the greatest cause to complain of themselves, and of their own disorder in governing. For their negligence in appointing no set work to their servants is the cause of everything being neglected. Where many things to be done are left to many servants, one will decline this, and another that, and say it does not belong to them. So the saying proves true that #emph[what is spoken to all is spoken to none,] and that general charges are no charges.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of masters overseeing the ways of their servants.]

The second direction is that masters exercise #emph[oversight] over their servants, and keep an eye on them, to see how they spend their time and carry out the business committed to them. The good mistress “watches over the ways of her household.”#footnote[Prov 31:27. Ruth 2:4.] This is noted of Boaz, that he went to his field to see what his reapers were doing.

The eye of a master is a great motive to make a servant diligent and faithful; for in this way he knows that both his diligence and his negligence will be seen, and dealt with accordingly. From this arose the proverb, #emph[The eye of the master makes the horse] #footnote[#emph[Oculus Domini pascit equum.] Matt 24:48.] #emph[fat.] The notion the wicked servant had, that his master was delaying his coming and so could not see what he did, made him as unfaithful as he was.

If a master’s position allows it, it is needful that he is present with his servants and goes before them as a good example. It is recorded of the good mistress that “she rises … and gives a #footnote[Prov 31:13, 15.] portion to her maidservants”: she is with them herself; she “works with eager hands.” This note of difference is set between a provident, thriving master and a dissolute, careless master: #emph[this man] says to his servants, #emph[You#super[+] go,] but #emph[that man] says, #emph[Let us go;] or, as it is put, #emph[Go, sirs,] and #emph[Let’s go, sirs.]

Contrary to this is letting everything go (as we say) #emph[at sixes and] #footnote[Contrary: carelessness.] #emph[sevens,] and never seeing what servants are doing. How can such masters expect their servants to be diligent in doing what serves their masters’ good, when they themselves are negligent in overseeing what serves their own good? Masters who give themselves to gambling, and to following their pastimes all day long, offend greatly in this. So also do mistresses who spend all the morning lying in bed and dressing themselves (a custom quite contrary to what is noted of the good mistress),#footnote[Prov 31:15.] and at noon, when they come out of their room, scold and quarrel because things are not further along.

=== §. 40. #emph[Of stirring up servants to their duty by both fair and foul means.]

The third direction is that masters use what means they can to urge and stir up their servants to be diligent and faithful: in the first place exhortation, admonition, persuasion, promises of reward and other fair means like these. Such were the means which Saul used to make his servants faithful to him, when he said, “Will the son of Jesse give all of you#super[+] fields and vineyards,” #footnote[1 Sam 22:7.] etc. If what he expected from them had been good, this way of dealing with them would have been commendable. But if fair means do not prevail, then masters may and ought to #footnote[See §. 13.] rebuke, threaten and correct their servants.

And if servants are brazen in sinning, and neither fair nor foul means will reclaim them, they must then be thrust out of doors. Note what the master in the parable said to his servant: “You cannot be steward any longer.”#footnote[Luke 16:2. Ps 101:7.] To the same purpose David also said, “No one who practices deceit shall dwell in my house; no one who tells lies shall stand in my presence.”

When Ishmael became a scoffer at God’s word, Abraham thrust #footnote[Gen 21:9. #emph[etc.]] him and his mother out of doors, and that by God’s appointment. The parable of cutting down the fruitless fig tree#footnote[Luke 13:7. #emph[etc.]] may fitly be applied to this point, and the reason also given there (“Why should it take up the ground?”). Why should incorrigible servants take up the room of good servants? Indeed, why should they remain to infect and pervert other servants?

#emph[Objection.] This is so far from helping servants in their livelihood that it may turn out to be their utter ruin.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Then they have no one justly to blame but themselves. Justice (in case of necessity) must take its course, though through the wickedness of man some harm follows from it.

2\. Carrying this out on some may make others look to themselves more carefully.

3\. It may make those who are thrust out more dutiful under another master, or more diligent in another course of life.

Contrary to this is the course of those who keep no course or order #footnote[A servant is not to be thrust out of doors for every offense.] in drawing their servants on to their duty, but begin with what should come last. Indeed, they use only the remedy which should not be used unless no other remedy will serve, which is to turn their servants out of doors on every small occasion. Many use no instruction, persuasion, admonition, rebuke or threatening when their servants have offended, but only this thunderbolt hurled at them: #emph[Be gone, get out of the house.] If this were recognized as a fault, servants might stay longer in a house than they ordinarily do, and master and servant might reap more good from each other; and indeed the secrets of a house would be better kept. For it is the frequent chopping and changing of servants that causes everything done privately in houses to be broadcast up and down.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of paying servants their wages.]

A third thing required of masters with regard to their servants’ livelihood is to give them their just wages. Even this is included in the general precept, “Pay everyone what you#super[+] owe him,”#footnote[Rom 13:7.] and more particularly expressed in the example of #footnote[Matt 20:8.] the master who hired laborers into his vineyard, and at the end of their work gave every one of them his wages. Indeed, there is an express #footnote[Deut 24:15.] law to this purpose.

#emph[Objection.] These places concern laborers hired by the day.

#emph[Answer.] Servants are in the same rank, and the ground for both is the same, for both work for wages. Indeed, the argument follows all the more strongly from the less to the greater, like this: if a laborer and servant for a day must have his wages justly paid, much more a servant and laborer for a year, and for years.

1\. A master’s agreement requires as much. If there were no other bond, yet the bond by which he voluntarily binds himself ties him to it in conscience. Among the fruits of the Spirit Saint Paul#footnote[Gal 5:22.] counts “faithfulness,” meaning by it trustworthiness in keeping promises and agreements.

2\. Common fairness and justice require as much, for wages are as due for labor as money for goods. Christ takes it as an undeniable principle that “the worker is worthy of his wages”;#footnote[Luke 10:7.] so also does #footnote[1 Tim 5:18.] the apostle.

This duty is to be performed to those who are hired for wages. Of those who are provided with everything by their master, I spoke #footnote[§. 23, 25.] before.

In giving servants their wages these three things are to be #footnote[Direction for giving wages.] observed.

1\. That sufficient wages are allowed: at least so much as may serve to provide such necessities as are fitting for a servant. For there is great reason that he who works should live by his work.

2\. That they are given in due season. #footnote[Lev 19:13. Deut 24:15.] God would not have the laborer’s pay left unpaid one night after it was due. The time agreed by a servant for his wages is the due time: he expects it then; he plans his uses of it for that time; at that time therefore it ought to be given him.

3\. That they are paid in full, according to the agreement. The master’s promise and the servant’s need require as much.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of masters’ injustice about their servants’ wages.]

Injustice contrary to the duty named above, of paying servants their wages duly, is committed in many ways.

1\. When masters withhold their servants’ wages altogether: #footnote[Deut 24:15. Jas 5:4.] this is a crying sin, which enters into the ears of God.

2\. When they make their servants ask for their wages again and again, even until they are ashamed; indeed, make them stay and wait for it until they are forced to sigh to God, or else to pilfer and steal to supply their needs. Though these masters have no intention of utterly defrauding their servants of their due, yet putting them off and delaying payment puts servants, who for the most part live only #emph[from hand to mouth,] into great straits. The Lord knew this well, and therefore #footnote[Lev 19:13. Deut 24:15.] expressly forbade the withholding of a servant’s wages #emph[one night.] This therefore is a matter not only of unkindness, but also of injustice.

3\. When masters alter and change the agreed wages, and seek to reduce them, as #footnote[Gen 31:41.] hard-hearted Laban did: by this they show that they begrudge their servants’ welfare and seek only themselves.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of allowing servants to provide for themselves.]

A fourth thing required of masters with regard to their servants’ livelihood is that, after sufficient service has been done, they allow their servants to provide for themselves. Jacob asked this of Laban as a most fair and reasonable thing, saying, “when shall I also provide for my own household?”#footnote[Gen 30:30.] (as was #footnote[§. 34.] noted before in general). This concerns especially such servants as have been with masters a long time as apprentices, and have spent their time, labor and pains solely and wholly for their masters’ good. #footnote[Exod 21:2. Deut 15:12.] God’s law made express provision for such, that after certain years of service they should go free. So also do the laws of our land, especially the ordinances of London.

Here lies a main difference between servants (who are under subjection, and kept to work for their own good) and beasts (which are only for man’s service and good).

Those therefore who are of a contrary mind, keeping servants #footnote[Contrary.] as long as they possibly can, even all their lives, at hard labor and in servitude, deserve to be served by beasts rather than by men and women. This severity of masters toward their servants is particularly noted to be #footnote[Jer 34:16, 17.] one of the causes of God’s great indignation against the Jews, by which he was provoked to give them over as captives to their enemies.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of kindness to be shown to good servants.]

As masters must give what is #emph[just] to all servants, so they must give what is #footnote[§. 20.] #emph[equal] to those who deserve it. That is, they must be of a corresponding mind and disposition toward good, upright, loving, kind, faithful servants, who do not stand so much on what is demanded of them as on what they are able to do for their masters, and for that reason, as they see occasion, often do much more than is demanded or expected. Goodness calls for goodness, good will for good will; and this is to #emph[do the same things.] This kind of #emph[Equity] consists in these and similar particulars.

1\. Masters must think well of such good servants, and hold them in high regard. Abram#footnote[Gen 15:2, 3.] counted his old, good, faithful servant as his child, and until he had a child thought of making him his heir. Great was the esteem which Potiphar #footnote[#emph[Qui vere patresfamilias sunt, seruis in familia sua tanquam filijs consulunt. Aug. de Ciu. Dei, l.] 19. #emph[c.] 16.] had for Joseph, when he raised him from a slave and put him “in charge of his household,” Gen 39:4. Why is the title #emph[father] given to masters (2 Kgs 5:13), and the title #emph[sons] to servants (Josh 7:19 and 1 Sam 24:16), but to show that servants should bear a childlike affection toward their masters, and that masters should bear a fatherly affection toward such servants?

2\. Masters must take notice of the goodness and kindness of such servants, and show that they do, both by giving them due praise and by a good reward. Both are noted in the pattern of that great master who said, “Well done, good #footnote[Matt 25:21.] and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things.” In this way those good servants will be all the more encouraged to hold on, and others will be moved to imitate them. The apostle gives this encouragement to all under authority: “do what is right, and #footnote[Rom 13:3.] you will have his praise.” This phrase implies that governors ought to praise those who do well.

3\. If such servants are accused of some heinous crime, masters must not rashly believe it, but rather sift and examine the matter thoroughly. Here Potiphar#footnote[Gen 39:19.] failed greatly, and by that means lost such a servant as he could never get again. If a good servant happens to slip and commit a fault, his master ought in wisdom either to take no notice of it, or to pass it over with some mild admonition, and not deal with him as with a wicked, graceless servant.

4\. When such servants depart (their agreed term having expired), their masters must not send them away “empty-handed,” #footnote[Deut 15:13.] but help them in their #emph[marriage] (as Moses’ master did)#footnote[Exod 2:21.] or in their setting up in business, as the great master did who put his wise and faithful servant “in charge of all his possessions.” #footnote[Matt 24:47.]

=== §. 45. #emph[Of unkind dealing with good servants.]

Those who are of a contrary mind are unworthy of good and kind servants; and many masters are of that mind:

1\. Some make no difference between servants, but think of bad and good all alike. They think that the best servants only do their duty, and that therefore no extraordinary regard is to be shown them. But it is a point of wisdom at times to count a duty as a kindness, especially when good will of heart is joined with the outward performance of duty.

2\. Others think it good policy to take no notice of any servant’s extraordinary faithfulness and diligence, to praise and reward it, for fear it should puff them up too much. But there is much more fear of servants fainting and growing weary of doing good if they have no encouragement, than of their growing insolent through encouragement.

3\. Others will be more ready to check and rebuke such servants for every slip, and for failing in anything, than other servants, because the others care less for their rebuke. In this they show a lack of wisdom in managing their authority well.

4\. Others, when their servants are about to leave, or to marry, or to set up in business, will look for some occasion or other to fall out with them, on purpose to send them away empty-handed. Many will show a pleasant face to profitable servants until the time of reward comes, and then begin to frown, as Laban #footnote[Gen 31:2.] did. Indeed, some masters are so far from seeking the prosperity of faithful, wise, diligent, skillful servants that they will hinder #footnote[#emph[Sic cum inferiore viuas, quemadmodum tecum superiorem velles viuere. Senec. epist.] 47.] them all they can, and keep them down, fearing that as their servants rise, they themselves will decline and fall. These are both unkind and ungrateful masters. Would masters want to be dealt with like this by their superiors? You ought to live with your inferior as you would have your superior live with you.

#emph[So much for masters’ duties. The reasons to move them to perform their duties follow.]
